e-gerund"],"skill":"despite-gerund","contextKey":"contrast-concession-47","fingerprint":"444705c10d4646d4057786bf81a387ba60e8c3c960edd23d2f370a2004780c25","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-contrast"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Despite is followed by a **noun phrase or -ing clause**.","After **despite**, use **installing** to express the conceded activity.","Choose **installing**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"A bare base verb does not follow despite in this pattern.","B":"Despite does not take a to-infinitive here.","C":"A past form alone does not form the required complement.","D":"After **despite**, use **installing** to express the conceded activity."}}},{"id":"19543bd8-50c5-59dd-aa94-19d29f049f74","topicSlug":"contrast-concession","level":"B2","prompt":"Choose the correct concession phrase: “____ the difficulty, we managed to install the new handrail.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"In spite of"},{"id":"B","text":"Despite of"},{"id":"C","text":"In spite"},{"id":"D","text":"Although of"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"Distinguish the fixed forms **despite** and **in spite of**.","explanation":"**In spite of** is the complete three-word phrase before the noun phrase.","keywords":["despite-of-error"],"skill":"despite-of-error","contextKey":"contrast-concession-47","fingerprint":"e9dcb3887ae4a6fa6a75c0898d54773d01c0daac46f817dd976b96ad48c589fe","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-contrast"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Distinguish the fixed forms **despite** and **in spite of**.","**In spite of** is the complete three-word phrase before the noun phrase.","Choose **In spite of**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**In spite of** is the complete three-word phrase before the noun phrase.","B":"Despite does not take of.","C":"In spite requires of before this noun phrase.","D":"Although introduces a clause and does not form although of."}}},{"id":"f8331a4d-acfd-5d37-ad67-d62c71b4a1d6","topicSlug":"contrast-concession","level":"B2","prompt":"Choose formal punctuation linking two complete clauses: “We installed the new handrail ____ however, the supervisor requested a change.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"of"},{"id":"B","text":";"},{"id":"C","text":","},{"id":"D","text":"no punctuation"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"However is a **linking adverb**; it does not make a comma alone sufficient.","explanation":"A **semicolon** separates the independent clauses before linking adverb however.","keywords":["however-punctuation"],"skill":"however-punctuation","contextKey":"contrast-concession-47","fingerprint":"91dd3c947579280e77c2da0d54a8fd943594e10a032cbb43b1db9a2a6721b96e","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-contrast"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["However is a **linking adverb**; it does not make a comma alone sufficient.","A **semicolon** separates the independent clauses before linking adverb however.","Choose **;**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Of cannot connect these independent clauses.","B":"A **semicolon** separates the independent clauses before linking adverb however.","C":"A comma alone would create a comma splice.","D":"The clauses would run together."}}},{"id":"7fff28e4-bdfd-5139-a768-a25a37179f2a","topicSlug":"contrast-concession","level":"B2","prompt":"“____ we had compared the survey results, the supervisor asked for another check.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Despite"},{"id":"B","text":"In spite"},{"id":"C","text":"Howev