 intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Despite does not take a to-infinitive here.","B":"A past form alone does not form the required complement.","C":"After **despite**, use **preparing** to express the conceded activity.","D":"A bare base verb does not follow despite in this pattern."}}},{"id":"d945b6c2-4949-527c-a37a-e96f341b638f","topicSlug":"contrast-concession","level":"B2","prompt":"Choose the correct concession phrase: “____ the difficulty, we managed to prepare the evening meal.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"In spite"},{"id":"B","text":"Although of"},{"id":"C","text":"In spite of"},{"id":"D","text":"Despite of"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"Distinguish the fixed forms **despite** and **in spite of**.","explanation":"**In spite of** is the complete three-word phrase before the noun phrase.","keywords":["despite-of-error"],"skill":"despite-of-error","contextKey":"contrast-concession-49","fingerprint":"5df98cf07e093e37d6b844b486bc79d5aa69ed09097759838ff33383fea9d375","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-contrast"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Distinguish the fixed forms **despite** and **in spite of**.","**In spite of** is the complete three-word phrase before the noun phrase.","Choose **In spite of**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"In spite requires of before this noun phrase.","B":"Although introduces a clause and does not form although of.","C":"**In spite of** is the complete three-word phrase before the noun phrase.","D":"Despite does not take of."}}},{"id":"630e4614-ab10-55da-a024-00fff529b51a","topicSlug":"contrast-concession","level":"B2","prompt":"Choose formal punctuation linking two complete clauses: “We prepared the evening meal ____ however, the supervisor requested a change.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":","},{"id":"B","text":"no punctuation"},{"id":"C","text":"of"},{"id":"D","text":";"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"However is a **linking adverb**; it does not make a comma alone sufficient.","explanation":"A **semicolon** separates the independent clauses before linking adverb however.","keywords":["however-punctuation"],"skill":"however-punctuation","contextKey":"contrast-concession-49","fingerprint":"672d7b86e5d7b57afb72d4e353bd092bf6f54ae5e0304cd1df9bbe84f1a3d778","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-contrast"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["However is a **linking adverb**; it does not make a comma alone sufficient.","A **semicolon** separates the independent clauses before linking adverb however.","Choose **;**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"A comma alone would create a comma splice.","B":"The clauses would run together.","C":"Of cannot connect these independent clauses.","D":"A **semicolon** separates the independent clauses before linking adverb however."}}},{"id":"bdaf5caa-d6fe-570c-a563-6e92c72c4f40","topicSlug":"contrast-concession","level":"B2","prompt":"“____ we had repaired the rowing boat, the supervisor asked for another check.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Although"},{"id":"B","text":"Despite"},{"id":"C","text":"In spite"},{"id":"D","text":"However"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"The contrast is expressed by a **full finite clause**.","explanation":"**Although** introduces a finite concession clause with subject we and verb had ... .","keywords":["although-clause"],"skill":"although-clause","contextKey":"contrast-concession-50","fingerprint":"deae7e6fbbceb4901236c2f89686b36bdf6efccb14b99457d084ea7ac08ae33e","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-contrast"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The contrast is expressed by a **full finite clause**.","**Although** introduces a finite concession clause with subject we and verb had ... .","Choose **Although**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**Although** introduces a finite concession clause with subject we and verb had ... .","B":"Despite normally takes a noun phrase or -ing expression, not this finite clause directly.","C":"The complete phrase is in spite of, and it still needs a suitable complement.","D":"However is a linking adverb and does not subordinate this clause in the same way."}}},{"id":"86ba24a3-fca3-578f-a027-e0836009aa61","topicSlug":"contrast-concession","level":"B2","prompt":"“Despite ____ the rowing boat, we still had plenty to do.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"repaired"},{"id":"B","text":"repairing"},{"id":"C","text":"repair"},{"id":"D","text":"to repair"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"Despite is followed by a **noun phrase or -ing clause**.","explanation":"After **despite**, use **repairing** to express the conceded activity.","keywords":["despite-gerund"],"skill":"despite-gerund","contextKey":"contrast-concession-50","fingerprint":"fa835f9c6d0a6bbb1707ee438d7ca43ab8f79cb8c77e3714ff5e506e751f018a","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-contrast"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Despite is followed by a **noun phrase or -ing clause**.","After **despite**, use **repairing** to express the conceded activity.","Choose **repairing**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"A past form alone does not form the required complement.","B":"After **despite**, use **repairing** to express the conceded activity.","C":"A bare base verb does not follow despite in this pattern.","D":"Despite does not take a to-infinitive here."}}},{"id":"d5c666bc-bc06-573a-a81e-6beaabcd301d","topicSlug":"contrast-concession","level":"B2","prompt":"Choose the correct concession phrase: “____ the difficulty, we managed to repair the rowing boat.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"In spite"},{"id":"B","text":"Although of"},{"id":"C","text":"In spite of"},{"id":"D","text":"Despite of"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"Distinguish the fixed forms **despite** and **in spite of**.","explanation":"**In spite of** is the complete three-word phrase before the noun phrase.","keywords":["despite-of-error"],"skill":"despite-of-error","contextKey":"contrast-concession-50","fingerprint":"8fbcc483013dd32569bf4e473715f15f73c2fb0aa204f0c8690ff392c2a9aff4","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-contrast"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Distinguish the fixed forms **despite** and **in spite of**.","**In spite of** is the complete three-word phrase before the noun phrase.","Choose **In spite of**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"In spite requires of before this noun phrase.","B":"Although introduces a clause and does not form although of.","C":"**In spite of** is the complete three-word phrase before the noun phrase.","D":"Despite does not take of."}}},{"id":"827e3927-eb99-5a68-a71d-b7e8d05c070e","topicSlug":"contrast-concession","level":"B2","prompt":"Choose formal punctuation linking two complete clauses: “We repaired the rowing boat ____ however, the supervisor requested a change.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"no punctuation"},{"id":"B","text":"of"},{"id":"C","text":";"},{"id":"D","text":","}],"answerId":"C","hint":"However is a **linking adverb**; it does not make a comma alone sufficient.","explanation":"A **semicolon** separates the independent clauses before linking adverb however.","keywords":["however-punctuation"],"skill":"however-punctuation","contextKey":"contrast-concession-50","fingerprint":"7e6a46e40a1f53d1ed32795b013f492d785d7f097d83fb63c3d50b56bc5f7041","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-contrast"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["However is a **linking adverb**; it does not make a comma alone sufficient.","A **semicolon** separates the independent clauses before linking adverb however.","Choose **;**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The clauses would run together.","B":"Of cannot connect these independent clauses.","C":"A **semicolon** separates the independent clauses before linking adverb however.","D":"A comma alone would create a comma splice."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Show that two ideas contrast, or that something happens in spite of an obstacle.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Although and even though","explanation":"Although, though and even though introduce a subordinate clause. They present information that contrasts with the main clause or might have led to a different expectation. Even though often makes the contrast stronger.","pattern":"**Although it rained**, we went out.","commonMistakes":["Do not normally combine although and but to mark the same relation in one standard sentence."],"examples":["“**Although** we had prepared the visitor guide, the supervisor asked for another check.”","“**Although** we had restored the old portrait, the supervisor asked for another check.”"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Despite and in spite of","explanation":"Despite and in spite of take a noun phrase or -ing expression. To follow them with a finite clause, use the fact that. Despite does not itself take of.","pattern":"**Despite the rain**, we went out. / **Despite the fact that it rained**, we went out.","commonMistakes":["Despite of is not the standard phrase; use despite or in spite of."],"examples":["“Despite **preparing** the visitor guide, we still had plenty to do.”","“Despite **restoring** the old portrait, we still had plenty to do.”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Whereas and while","explanation":"Whereas and contrastive while compare two facts, preferences or situations. While also has a time meaning, so ensure that context makes the contrast clear. Whereas is particularly useful when balancing one fact against another.","pattern":"She likes tea, **whereas** he prefers coffee.","commonMistakes":["Whereas normally introduces a clause, not a noun phrase by itself."],"examples":["Choose the correct concession phrase: “**In spite of** the difficulty, we managed to prepare the visitor guide.”","Choose the correct concession phrase: “**In spite of** the difficulty, we managed to restore the old portrait.”"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"However and nevertheless","explanation":"However and nevertheless are linking adverbs, not ordinary coordinating conjunctions. They can start a new sentence or follow a semicolon between independent clauses. Their punctuation differs from but even when the contrast is similar.","pattern":"It rained**; however,** we went out.","commonMistakes":["It rained, however we went out is a comma splice in ordinary formal prose."],"examples":["Choose formal punctuation linking two complete clauses: “We prepared the visitor guide **;** however, the supervisor requested a change.”","Choose formal punctuation linking two complete clauses: “We restored the old portrait **;** however, the supervisor requested a change.”"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["**Although it rained**, we went out.","**Despite the rain**, we went out. / **Despite the fact that it rained**, we went out.","She likes tea, **whereas** he prefers coffee.","It rained**; however,** we went out."]},"stage":"4 · Connecting meanings","prerequisites":["linking-clauses"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
